window.docContents[59].resolve({"/The-standard-library/Interface/#Core-C______--The-standard-library--Interface":{"contents":"A module  gives at most five static relations and four dynamic ones. Each is an instance of a judgment of the language, with  the template arguments of the instance. A relation the entity does not have is left at .| Judgment | Meaning | Lean |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | the instance is well formed | wf |\n|  | a signature of new | new |\n|  | the signature of indexing, a location | index |\n|  | an instance is deleted | delete |\n|  | the signature of a function without a body | call |\n|  | the creation of an instance | new |\n|  | the location of an element | index |\n|  | the store after the instance leaves it | delete |\n|  | the call of a function without a body | call |The static relations form StaticModule and the dynamic ones Module, found by the declared name through staticOf and moduleOf. The functions StaticFns and Fns implement them, found through staticFnsOf and fnsOf. Each dynamic function names the rule it concludes, for the trace. The relation wf is the predicate  of the rule WF-Lib of . The module of assert leaves it at , since assert is no type.An object type is a class or an entity of the library whose module gives new a signature, Std.isObject. Its values live in the store and are reached by pointer or by reference, so no variable, field, parameter or result has the type by value. A vector and a std::function are objects.\n\nThe rules of the language for a subject of the library take the relation of its module as a premise. The arguments are values, evaluated left to right, and a module premise with no derivation makes the use error in the evaluator.The receiver is evaluated before the index, the order C++17 fixes for subscripting (N4659 §8.2.1 ¶1), also when operator[] is overloaded (N4659 §16.3.1.2 ¶2). In a value position e[i] is read by the rule Read.The command delete e on a pointer to an instance frees what the module owns and then the location of the instance.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009The standard library","header":"8.1. Interface","id":"/The-standard-library/Interface/#Core-C______--The-standard-library--Interface"}});